The chart law $F_x$ with $v_1=x$, $v_2=1$, and $v_{i>2}=0$ gives compatible presentations $$\label{geom:quotient-thickenings}
 \mathcal M_k/(t^d)=[\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}R_d/G_{\mathrm{up}}],\qquad
 \mathcal N/(t^d)=[\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}R_d/G].$$ Under these presentations, $\mathcal X_k/(t^d)$ is $[\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}R_d/\Lambda]$. Sheaves are smooth, that is, discrete continuous, semilinear representations over $R_d$. The compatible coefficient action on $R$ satisfies $$g(x)\in x+x^2R\quad(g\in H),\qquad
 \lambda(x)=\lambda^{-1}x\quad(\lambda\in\Lambda).$$ If $u$ is the chart basis of the weight line with $t=xu$, then $$\label{geom:twist-cocycle}
 g(u)=\frac{x}{g(x)}u\quad(g\in H),\qquad
 \lambda(u)=\lambda u\quad(\lambda\in\Lambda).$$ For every strict label representing an element $g\in H$, there is a formal isomorphism $F_{g(x)}\to F_x$ over $R$ reducing to that label.

The filtration is complete and separated, and $$\label{geom:group-graded}
 H(i)/H(i+1)=
 \begin{cases}
 (k,+),&i\text{ odd},\\
 (k/\mathbb F_p,+),&i\text{ even},
 \end{cases}
 \qquad [H(i),H(j)]\subset H(i+j).$$ The $p$-th-power map carries each graded digit to the identical digit two indices later. Choose $g_1,g_2$ with first digits an $\mathbb F_p$-basis of $k$, and choose $g_3=\gamma$ with only nonzero digit $t_2=\theta\notin\mathbb F_p$. Then $$(a_1,a_2,a_3)\longmapsto g_1^{a_1}g_2^{a_2}g_3^{a_3}$$ gives unique coordinates $\mathbb Z_p^3\to H$. Modulo $\Gamma_s$, the coordinates are taken modulo $p^s$. In particular $\Gamma_s$ consists of coordinates divisible by $p^s$, and $H(2)$ consists of coordinates whose first two entries are divisible by $p$. The element $\gamma$ commutes with $\Lambda$.

Choose an affine law chart $l:S\to\mathcal M_k$, set $I=l_*l^*$, and let $J$ be the cokernel of its unit. The two-term totalization $$\label{geom:Q-definition}
 Q=\bigl[r_*I\longrightarrow r_*J\bigr],
 \qquad\text{in homological degrees }0,-1,$$ computes $Rr_*$ on twisted periodic complexes and preserves quasi-isomorphisms. There are natural identifications $$QT_k\simeq T',\qquad
 \pi_*(r^*U)\cong\pi_*(Qr^*U)\quad(U\in\mathbf D(\mathcal N)),$$ and natural triangles $$\label{geom:central-triangle}
 U\longrightarrow Qr^*U\longrightarrow U[-1]
                     \longrightarrow U[1].$$

The functor $L$ is the flat affine restriction and pushforward for the open $t\ne 0$, whose derived category it embeds fully faithfully. Completion is exact and idempotent. Its values are right orthogonal, in every shift, to $t$-invertible objects. For every $P$, the fiber of $P\to\widehat P$ is $t$-invertible, and there are natural equivalences $$P/t^d\simeq\widehat P/t^d \qquad(d\geq 1).$$ The following square is a homotopy pullback: $$\label{complete:fracture-square}
\begin{tikzcd}[column sep=large,row sep=large]
 P \arrow[r] \arrow[d] & \widehat P \arrow[d]\\
 LP \arrow[r] & L\widehat P.
\end{tikzcd}$$ Thus the original object is recovered by matching its open and complete parts over the localized completion. Moreover, $$\label{complete:localization-sections}
 \pi_i LP=\mathop{\mathrm{colim}}_{s\geq 0}\pi_{i+sD}P.$$

There is an identification of graded algebras $$\label{complete:operation-algebra}
 \mathscr E_{sD}=(x^{-s}\Delta)^\Lambda\quad(s\in\mathbb Z),
 \qquad
 \mathscr E_n=0\quad(n\notin D\mathbb Z).$$ Products use left-coefficient skew convolution in $\Delta[x^{-1}]$, with degrees added. Every element of $\mathscr E_{sD}$ has a unique compatible system of ordinary sheaf-map representatives $T'_d\to T'_d(s)$, and these representatives realize multiplication strictly. Under <a href="#complete:operation-algebra" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="complete:operation-algebra">[complete:operation-algebra]</a>, the operation $t$ of degree $D$ is the coefficient $1\in x^{-1}\Delta$; it is central in $\mathscr E$.*


*Put $$A_{\mathrm{op}}=(\Delta[x^{-1}])^\Lambda.$$ With a formal $t$ recording degree $D$, localization identifies $$\label{complete:localized-operations}
 \mathscr E[t^{-1}]=A_{\mathrm{op}}[t^{\pm1}].$$

If $Y=\mathcal R(\mathsf P)$ for a perfect right dg $\mathscr E$-module $\mathsf P$, then naturally $$\label{complete:tensor-formulas}
 \begin{split}
 \pi_*Y&=H_*\bigl(\mathsf P\otimes^{\mathbf L}_{\mathscr E}
                           \mathsf L\bigr),\\
 W(Y)&=H_*\bigl(\mathsf P\otimes^{\mathbf L}_{\mathscr E}
                           \Phi[t^{\pm1}]\bigr).
 \end{split}$$ For a finite-cell model the ordinary cellular tensor computes each derived tensor. The actions in these formulas are the left actions of <a href="#complete:action-on-sections" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="complete:action-on-sections">[complete:action-on-sections]</a>.

For $m\ge0$, the finite measure $$D_m=\sum_{i=0}^m
 \frac{x^{r'_m}}{\prod_{0\le j\le m,\ j\ne i}(u_i-u_j)}
 \,\delta_{b_i^H}
 \label{op:measure}$$ belongs to $\Delta$. Its moments satisfy $$\int_H D_m(g)u_g^n=
 \begin{cases}
 0,&0\le n<m,\\
 x^{r'_m},&n=m,
 \end{cases}
 \qquad
 \int_H D_m(g)u_g^n\in x^{r'_m+n-m}R\quad(n>m).$$

An integer $j$ is an unmatched column of $a$ if and only if there is a vector in $\mathcal K$ with leading term $w^j$. An integer $l$ is a matched row if and only if it is the leading position of a nonzero vector in $a\Phi$. Moreover, for every $J\in\mathbb Z$, $$\dim_k\frac{a\Phi\cap F_J\Phi}{a(F_J\Phi)}
   =\#\{(l,j)\text{ matched}:l\le J<j\}<\infty.
 \label{glue:crossing-quotient}$$ The image $a\Phi$ is closed. Thus $\mathcal Q$ and $\mathcal K$ are Hausdorff locally compact $k$-vector spaces, with compact open subspaces given respectively by the images of $F_J\Phi$ and by $\mathcal K\cap F_J\Phi$.

For an idempotent-complete triangulated category $\mathcal C$ and an object $T_0$, every $P\in\mathop{\mathrm{Thick}}_{\mathcal C}(T_0)$ is a retract of an object $P_0$ admitting a finite tower of triangles $$F_j\longrightarrow P_j\longrightarrow P_{j+1}
      \longrightarrow F_j[1],
 \qquad 0\leq j<s,\qquad P_s=0,$$ where each $F_j$ is a finite sum of arbitrary integer shifts of $T_0$.
